00:40:19.248 --> 00:40:22.688
companies to operate as commercial businesses, and designed an

761
00:40:22.708 --> 00:40:24.628
accountability regime around that.

762
00:40:25.568 --> 00:40:27.888
Exclusion from LogOIMA was not an oversight.

763
00:40:28.548 --> 00:40:32.108
It was intentional and reflects the reality of port operations.

764
00:40:33.308 --> 00:40:37.088
Secondly, and I want to be very clear here, the ports are

765
00:40:37.168 --> 00:40:38.928
not resistant to transparency.

766
00:40:39.748 --> 00:40:43.648
We support the objective of strong public accountability, but we do not

767
00:40:43.668 --> 00:40:47.228
believe the bill has demonstrated that the existing accountability

768
00:40:47.328 --> 00:40:49.188
framework is materially failing.

769
00:40:50.428 --> 00:40:54.208
And thirdly, should it be concluded that even greater

770
00:40:54.248 --> 00:40:55.838
transparency is desirable,

771
00:40:56.838 --> 00:41:00.508
we do not believe this legislation will deliver the transparency or public

772
00:41:00.568 --> 00:41:02.108
understanding you are seeking.

773
00:41:03.807 --> 00:41:06.988
So I'd like to begin with the legislative context.

774
00:41:06.998 --> 00:41:09.728
The current framework is not an accident.

775
00:41:09.758 --> 00:41:13.698
The Port Companies Act states that the principal objective of every port

776
00:41:13.748 --> 00:41:16.328
company is to operate as a successful business.

777
00:41:16.948 --> 00:41:20.828
So ports compete with each other for freight, they

778
00:41:20.888 --> 00:41:24.328
compete for shipping services, they negotiate commercial contracts,

779
00:41:24.928 --> 00:41:27.168
make large long-term infrastructure investments,

780
00:41:28.028 --> 00:41:31.948
and they increasingly compete internationally as part of global supply

781
00:41:32.048 --> 00:41:32.408
chains.

782
00:41:33.568 --> 00:41:37.508
Parliament recognized that publicly owned ports could remain publicly accountable

783
00:41:37.548 --> 00:41:41.038
while operating under commercial disciplines, rather than as

784
00:41:41.088 --> 00:41:43.908
extensions of local government administration.

785
00:41:43.948 --> 00:41:45.768
This bill would alter that balance.

786
00:41:46.828 --> 00:41:50.188
In our view, such a change should be supported by a clearly

787
00:41:50.348 --> 00:41:54.228
identified policy problem and evidence that the existing framework

788
00:41:54.268 --> 00:41:57.128
is no longer achieving Parliament's original intent.

789
00:41:57.848 --> 00:42:00.628
We are not persuaded that that threshold has been met.

790
00:42:02.288 --> 00:42:06.208
The port sector fully accepts that public accountability matters.

791
00:42:06.688 --> 00:42:10.067
The question is not whether ports should be accountable, they already are.

792
00:42:11.188 --> 00:42:14.738
As our written submission sets out, ports already operate within

793
00:42:14.788 --> 00:42:18.578
extensive accountability arrangements through shareholder oversight, company law,

794
00:42:18.748 --> 00:42:22.248
environmental regulation, health and safety regulation, maritime

795
00:42:22.288 --> 00:42:26.128
regulation, continuous disclosure obligations for listed companies,

796
00:42:26.668 --> 00:42:29.448
and a range of existing public reporting requirements.

797
00:42:30.548 --> 00:42:34.408
If you consider that there are genuine accountability gaps, we would welcome that

798
00:42:34.428 --> 00:42:38.408
discussion. But we do not believe that the bill clearly identifies where the

799
00:42:38.468 --> 00:42:41.968
existing framework has failed or why changing the model

800
00:42:42.068 --> 00:42:44.798
established by Parliament is the appropriate response

801
00:42:46.044 --> 00:42:49.664
More information does not necessarily produce more transparency.

802
00:42:50.604 --> 00:42:54.204
The first reading debate focused appropriately on transparency.

803
00:42:54.924 --> 00:42:58.834
We are not resistant to transparency, but transparency is not

804
00:42:58.884 --> 00:43:02.604
an end in itself. Its purpose is to improve accountability and

805
00:43:02.644 --> 00:43:06.424
public understanding. The committee should therefore ask not

806
00:43:06.464 --> 00:43:10.434
simply whether the bill might produce more information, it should ask whether it

807
00:43:10.464 --> 00:43:12.064
will produce better understanding.

808
00:43:13.064 --> 00:43:16.524
Legoyma provides access to information held by individual

809
00:43:16.534 --> 00:43:20.444
organizations, but many of the questions you raised are not questions

810
00:43:20.464 --> 00:43:24.104
about individual port companies, they're questions about the freight

811
00:43:24.184 --> 00:43:25.344
system as a whole.

812
00:43:26.284 --> 00:43:28.724
Are infrastructure investments properly coordinated?

813
00:43:29.304 --> 00:43:33.004
Is the network resilient? Do we have the right balance between pricing,

814
00:43:33.144 --> 00:43:36.654
resilience, and competition? Do ports, rail, and shipping

815
00:43:36.764 --> 00:43:38.594
services work together effectively?

816
00:43:39.144 --> 00:43:42.844
These are all legitimate questions, but they cannot be answered by

817
00:43:42.864 --> 00:43:46.144
requesting additional documents from one port company at a time.

818
00:43:47.274 --> 00:43:50.904
The challenge is not simply access to information, it is bringing

819
00:43:50.943 --> 00:43:54.714
information together in a way that explains how the national freight system is

820
00:43:54.744 --> 00:43:55.264
performing.

821
00:43:56.344 --> 00:43:59.944
The bill may produce more information, but it's much less likely to produce more

822
00:43:59.984 --> 00:44:00.684
understanding.

823
00:44:01.924 --> 00:44:05.884
Indeed, there is a real risk that it produces fragmented information about

824
00:44:05.894 --> 00:44:09.624
individual organizations without providing parliament or the

825
00:44:09.664 --> 00:44:13.234
public with a coherent picture of the system that they are

826
00:44:13.304 --> 00:44:17.184
actually interested in. Several members during the first reading

827
00:44:17.244 --> 00:44:21.044
debate also referred to broader issues such as national freight strategy,

828
00:44:21.544 --> 00:44:25.244
resilience, environmental performance, and the future role of ports.

829
00:44:26.004 --> 00:44:29.184
These are important issues, but they reinforce the point.

830
00:44:29.534 --> 00:44:32.444
These are system-level policy questions.

831
00:44:33.524 --> 00:44:37.224
A requester-driven information regime applied to individual commercial

832
00:44:37.284 --> 00:44:40.944
entities is not a substitute for a coherent national freight

833
00:44:41.044 --> 00:44:43.984
strategy or nationally consistent reporting.

834
00:44:45.324 --> 00:44:48.584
Similarly, the debate referred to expectations around environmental

835
00:44:48.644 --> 00:44:50.844
responsibility and being a reasonable employer.

836
00:44:51.444 --> 00:44:54.984
These are important objectives, but they are fundamentally

837
00:44:55.043 --> 00:44:58.694
questions about how organizations should behave, not about access to

838
00:44:58.764 --> 00:44:59.404
information.

839
00:45:00.344 --> 00:45:03.824
If Parliament wishes to strengthen those obligations, they are best addressed

840
00:45:03.833 --> 00:45:07.484
directly through the legislation governing those matters rather than

841
00:45:07.544 --> 00:45:09.504
through an information access regime.

842
00:45:10.884 --> 00:45:11.104
So,

843
00:45:12.224 --> 00:45:15.944
as I said, port CEOs' group is not arguing against greater transparency.

844
00:45:16.484 --> 00:45:20.173
If you conclude that additional transparency would improve public confidence,

845
00:45:20.464 --> 00:45:23.854
we would welcome working constructively on a more targeted approach.

846
00:45:24.984 --> 00:45:28.924
In our view, you should first identify the accountability

847
00:45:28.944 --> 00:45:32.924
gap you wish to address, then consider what information would genuinely

848
00:45:32.964 --> 00:45:36.184
improve public understanding, and then finally, design a framework capable of

849
00:45:36.204 --> 00:45:39.984
delivering that outcome. In our view, that is more likely to

850
00:45:40.044 --> 00:45:43.664
achieve this bill's underlying objective than extending a

851
00:45:43.744 --> 00:45:46.824
requester-driven regime to individual port companies.

852
00:45:47.604 --> 00:45:51.564
For those reasons, we respectfully recommend the bill does not

853
00:45:51.624 --> 00:45:53.464
proceed in its current form. Thank you.

854
00:45:53.984 --> 00:45:57.024
Thank you. We've got about four minutes left, but I think we will go a fraction

855
00:45:57.064 --> 00:45:58.344
over here because I know there'll be a few questions.

856
00:45:58.384 --> 00:46:00.094
So Tracey first, then Dan, then I've got some.

857
00:46:00.224 --> 00:46:02.414
Thank you. And you ended up probably answering most of my questions.

858
00:46:02.414 --> 00:46:03.224
Oh, okay. Well, no, that's fine.

859
00:46:03.384 --> 00:46:05.364
But I think I've still got kind of one.

860
00:46:06.024 --> 00:46:09.184
And look, I do have a lot of sympathy for the transparency v

861
00:46:09.244 --> 00:46:12.384
accountability dichotomy that exists.

862
00:46:12.953 --> 00:46:12.953
Yeah.

863
00:46:12.964 --> 00:46:15.924
But if the CE sector is already sufficient as

864
00:46:16.864 --> 00:46:19.584
affordability, are you able to identify any

865
00:46:20.464 --> 00:46:24.244
information that the public can currently directly get from port companies

866
00:46:24.284 --> 00:46:28.044
about things like major strategic, environmental safety or

867
00:46:28.084 --> 00:46:31.024
employment sort of decisions? And how do they obtain that?

868
00:46:31.544 --> 00:46:34.994
Yep. So there's a number of ways that they can get those sorts of bits of

869
00:46:35.044 --> 00:46:38.644
information. So each of the ports publish their statement of corporate intent.

870
00:46:38.744 --> 00:46:40.784
They publish annual reports.

871
00:46:41.424 --> 00:46:44.924
In the case of the listed companies, they're responsible to

872
00:46:45.024 --> 00:46:46.244
shareholders and AGMs.

873
00:46:47.204 --> 00:46:47.464
But

874
00:46:48.404 --> 00:46:52.384
in the case of those that aren't listed, they front up to their council

875
00:46:52.464 --> 00:46:54.204
owners, and those are public meetings.

876
00:46:54.524 --> 00:46:57.764
People are able to watch those and attend those,

877
00:46:58.824 --> 00:47:02.344
and ask questions in some cases. And

878
00:47:04.243 --> 00:47:07.923
so there's a number of different ways that people can get access to

879
00:47:07.984 --> 00:47:11.664
information. I've worked in a number of different sectors, so I'm

880
00:47:11.704 --> 00:47:14.944
relatively new to the sector. Thank you for the congratulations earlier.

881
00:47:15.744 --> 00:47:17.084
I've got to say, I'm absolutely,

882
00:47:18.024 --> 00:47:21.764
to use a Liverpool colloquialism, gobsmacked by the level

883
00:47:21.904 --> 00:47:25.694
of operational information that is made publicly

884
00:47:25.694 --> 00:47:29.054
available about the ports. Compared to other sectors that I've worked in,

885
00:47:29.664 --> 00:47:33.574
there is vast amounts of data and information out there and available,

886
00:47:34.704 --> 00:47:37.384
whether it's published through the Ministry of Transport or through other

887
00:47:37.444 --> 00:47:38.144
mechanisms.

888
00:47:39.864 --> 00:47:41.964
I actually think we've got the inverse problem.

889
00:47:42.004 --> 00:47:44.634
We've got so much data, it's actually hard, and I'm sure you've found this as

890
00:47:44.664 --> 00:47:47.684
you've worked through pulling your select committee report.

891
00:47:48.004 --> 00:47:50.964
There's so much information out there, it's actually hard to distill themes from

892
00:47:51.024 --> 00:47:51.164
it.

893
00:47:52.404 --> 00:47:52.924
And you can

894
00:47:53.844 --> 00:47:56.544
pretty much find a point of data to justify any

895
00:47:57.424 --> 00:47:58.784
story that you want to tell.

896
00:48:00.164 --> 00:48:03.644
So I think the big gap actually is not

897
00:48:04.044 --> 00:48:07.804
port by port, that depth of information, it's more how does our whole

898
00:48:07.864 --> 00:48:08.874
system hang together.

899
00:48:10.064 --> 00:48:13.764
So if there's a disruption at one port, how does that then flow through to the rest

900
00:48:13.804 --> 00:48:16.324
of the ports? And it's actually quite hard to see that picture.

901
00:48:16.704 --> 00:48:20.324
I think that's the gap, and I don't think Legoyma would help us understand that any

902
00:48:20.364 --> 00:48:20.724
better.

903
00:48:20.864 --> 00:48:22.104
What is the fix for that then?

904
00:48:23.424 --> 00:48:23.724
So

905
00:48:24.624 --> 00:48:28.064
this is something I've picked up and I'm working with the CEs to try and

906
00:48:28.824 --> 00:48:32.164
bring together some reporting that we put out ourselves,

907
00:48:33.384 --> 00:48:35.064
and we can work with the officials on that.

908
00:48:35.104 --> 00:48:37.444
One of the reasons why it's hard to

909
00:48:38.444 --> 00:48:42.424
get a consistent picture is each of the ports over time have evolved

910
00:48:42.464 --> 00:48:43.584
their own way of measuring,

911
00:48:44.564 --> 00:48:45.604
right? So

912
00:48:46.676 --> 00:48:50.516
Whether a vessel has arrived on time in one port is measured one way

913
00:48:50.796 --> 00:48:53.355
and is measured a different way at a different port.

914
00:48:53.416 --> 00:48:55.916
So we need to work together to get consistent on that.

915
00:48:56.996 --> 00:49:00.716
So I think that's an opportunity. I think it will help everybody

916
00:49:00.736 --> 00:49:04.556
understand how the network as a whole performs as we move

917
00:49:04.616 --> 00:49:08.456
into the discussions around evolving a port strategy

918
00:49:08.476 --> 00:49:11.476
and things like that. Yeah. A lot of my career was spent

919
00:49:11.956 --> 00:49:14.216
in telco, actually. So

920
00:49:15.016 --> 00:49:18.516
the comparison I see is you can see how little bits of the

921
00:49:18.916 --> 00:49:21.636
