makes that relationship even more imbalanced than it is currently.

958
00:51:15.416 --> 00:51:19.146
So we have to remember that these are, in many cases,

959
00:51:19.316 --> 00:51:22.736
giant commercial organizations that are incredibly well-resourced.

960
00:51:23.396 --> 00:51:24.096
So there's that.

961
00:51:25.336 --> 00:51:26.896
Another thing that

962
00:51:27.836 --> 00:51:31.036
I would suggest the committee should bear in mind is the chilling effect

963
00:51:31.976 --> 00:51:35.896
that counterparties, whoever they may be,

964
00:51:35.996 --> 00:51:39.916
will share at the moment their commercial information with ports

965
00:51:39.976 --> 00:51:43.856
about shipping forecasts and predicted

966
00:51:43.876 --> 00:51:45.316
freight volumes and so forth.

967
00:51:46.116 --> 00:51:49.696
Now, those could be carved out with the commercial exemption,

968
00:51:50.416 --> 00:51:53.716
but if you're one of those counterparties, you may choose not to share that,

969
00:51:55.736 --> 00:51:59.656
because you don't know exactly how it'll be treated, and that will also hurt the

970
00:51:59.676 --> 00:52:02.856
efficiency and the ability of the ports to operate commercially.

971
00:52:03.356 --> 00:52:05.476
So I think those are the key things.

972
00:52:05.516 --> 00:52:09.276
Yes, there's an administrative burden, but it's unbalancing

973
00:52:09.616 --> 00:52:11.556
what's already some quite

974
00:52:12.396 --> 00:52:16.256
challenging commercial relationships with some very well-resourced

975
00:52:16.316 --> 00:52:19.856
global companies. And there's the risk of the chilling effect

976
00:52:20.196 --> 00:52:20.696
as well.

977
00:52:21.016 --> 00:52:21.416
Thank you.

978
00:52:22.356 --> 00:52:25.536
And the question from me, particularly, I don't know if you read the

